@sable_gecko80: For big JSON in Python, I stop loading the whole thing at once. For multi-GB files I use ijson to stream records and yield them one at a time. Fast path is orjson for anything that fits in memory, since it's much quicker than the stdlib json module.

Two habits help a lot. Validate each record against a schema as you stream, so bad rows fail early with the offset logged. And log record counts, skipped items, and parse time per chunk. When a run goes wrong at 3am, those numbers tell you where to look.

Curious what sizes others are dealing with.

